  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Asthma exacerbation with a cold (J45.31)</content>. A mild-to-moderate exacerbation triggered by a viral cold: peak flow 68 percent of her best, rising to 82 percent after treatment, with normal oxygen saturation. Rapid influenza and COVID-19 tests are negative. She does not need an oral corticosteroid now, because she responded well and her peak flow is above 60 percent; no antibiotic, because there is no sign of bacterial infection. [S]<list><item>Step up to maintenance and reliever: budesonide-formoterol one inhalation twice daily and one as needed, up to 12 a day, through the winter.</item><item>Standby prednisone 40 mg daily for five days only if peak flow falls below 240 L/min or does not improve within 48 hours, with a call to the clinic.</item><item>Emergency care or 911 if peak flow falls below 160 L/min, she cannot speak in sentences, or the reliever does not help.</item><item>Nurse telephone call in two days; clinic review within a week.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Asthma (J45.30)</content>. Cold, damp weather and a cold are her main triggers, as they were last winter. [S]<list><item>Influenza and COVID-19 vaccines for the new season at the review, once the cold has passed.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Global Strategy for Asthma Management and Prevention, 2025 update (2025); 2020 Focused Updates to the Asthma Management Guidelines: A Report from the National Asthma Education and Prevention Program Coordinating Committee Expert Panel Working Group (2020)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Nurse telephone call in two days. Clinic review within a week. Emergency care for any red-zone sign. Next scheduled visit 1773-10-26: asthma review after an exacerbation with a cold on her return from abroad: control, repeat spirometry and FeNO, the winter plan, completion of vaccines, and her changed circumstances. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
